Passage 4: The Ancient Silk Road (Soshum)', true),
('8884a4f6-f89e-5bd1-b0d3-e8816281593f', 'LBE', 176, '04. Restatement & Paraphrasing (Part 1)', 'Sulit', 'Passage 4 (Questions 16–20) — Tema: Soshum (Sejarah Ekonomi & Globalisasi Awal)

Long before the advent of modern container ships and fiber-optic cables, the ancient Silk Road served as humanity''s premier transcontinental network of trade and cultural exchange. Stretching over 6,400 kilometers from East Asia to the Mediterranean basin, this interconnected web of overland caravan trails and maritime routes facilitated the movement of luxury commodities such as Chinese silk, Indian spices, and Roman glassware. Rather than being a single paved highway, the Silk Road operated as a dynamic relay system where merchants traded goods across regional hubs.

Beyond commercial merchandise, the Silk Road functioned as a vital conduit for the diffusion of technologies, philosophies, and scientific knowledge. Paper-making and gunpowder techniques traveled westward from China, fundamentally transforming European literacy and warfare, while mathematical concepts and astronomical instruments moved eastward. Simultaneously, major world religions—including Buddhism, Islam, and Nestorian Christianity—spread along these trade arteries, fostering cosmopolitan cities where diverse languages and traditions coexisted.

However, this unprecedented interconnectedness also carried unforeseen biological and geopolitical consequences. In the fourteenth century, the same trade routes that transported silk and spices inadvertently accelerated the westward spread of the bubonic plague, devastating populations across Asia and Europe. Furthermore, as maritime navigation technologies advanced in the fifteenth century, European powers sought direct sea routes to Asia to bypass overland middlemen, gradually diminishing the economic dominance of traditional Silk Road caravan cities.

Today, historians view the Silk Road as an early blueprint for globalization, demonstrating how commercial interdependence can drive both human innovation and shared vulnerability. Studying its rise and transformation reminds modern societies that global connectivity brings immense cultural enrichment alongside complex systemic risks.

Source: Adapted from UNESCO Silk Roads Programme & Frankopan, P., "The Silk Roads: A New History of the World," 2021.

Which of the following best restates the sentence "Rather than being a single paved highway, the Silk Road operated as a dynamic relay system where merchants traded goods across regional hubs." in the first paragraph?', 'The Silk Road consisted of one continuous paved road where individual merchants traveled directly from China to Rome without stopping.', 'Instead of a solitary constructed road, the Silk Road functioned as an adaptable network in which traders exchanged merchandise through various regional centers.', 'Regional hubs along the Silk Road prevented merchants from trading luxury commodities across different territories.', 'Paved highways were built across East Asia to replace the inefficient relay system used by ancient Silk Road merchants.', 'Merchants avoided regional hubs because the dynamic relay system made overland trade too dangerous.', 'B', 'Instead of a solitary constructed road, the Silk Road functioned as an adaptable network in which traders exchanged merchandise through various regional centers.
Alasan Jawaban Benar: "Rather than being a single paved highway" $\rightarrow$ "Instead of a solitary constructed road", dan "dynamic relay system where merchants traded goods across regional hubs" $\rightarrow$ "adaptable network in which traders exchanged merchandise through various regional centers".
Perbandingan dengan Opsi Lain:
(A) Salah: Bertentangan dengan teks yang menegaskan Jalur Sutra bukan satu jalan raya beraspal tunggal.
(C) Salah: Pusat-pusat regional (regional hubs) justru menjadi tempat pertukaran barang, bukan penghalang.
(D) Salah: Tidak ada pembangunan jalan raya beraspal untuk menggantikan sistem estafet.
(E) Salah: Pedagang justru memanfaatkan pusat regional tersebut untuk berdagang.', true),
('b232abed-1ab8-594e-b40e-ca235d9165ab', 'LBE', 177, '04. Restatement & Paraphrasing (Part 1)', 'Sulit', 'Passage 4 (Questions 16–20) — Tema: Soshum (Sejarah Ekonomi & Globalisasi Awal)

Long before the advent of modern container ships and fiber-optic cables, the ancient Silk Road served as humanity''s premier transcontinental network of trade and cultural exchange. Stretching over 6,400 kilometers from East Asia to the Mediterranean basin, this interconnected web of overland caravan trails and maritime routes facilitated the movement of luxury commodities such as Chinese silk, Indian spices, and Roman glassware. Rather than being a single paved highway, the Silk Road operated as a dynamic relay system where merchants traded goods across regional hubs.

Beyond commercial merchandise, the Silk Road functioned as a vital conduit for the diffusion of technologies, philosophies, and scientific knowledge. Paper-making and gunpowder techniques traveled westward from China, fundamentally transforming European literacy and warfare, while mathematical concepts and astronomical instruments moved eastward. Simultaneously, major world religions—including Buddhism, Islam, and Nestorian Christianity—spread along these trade arteries, fostering cosmopolitan cities where diverse languages and traditions coexisted.

However, this unprecedented interconnectedness also carried unforeseen biological and geopolitical consequences. In the fourteenth century, the same trade routes that transported silk and spices inadvertently accelerated the westward spread of the bubonic plague, devastating populations across Asia and Europe. Furthermore, as maritime navigation technologies advanced in the fifteenth century, European powers sought direct sea routes to Asia to bypass overland middlemen, gradually diminishing the economic dominance of traditional Silk Road caravan cities.

Today, historians view the Silk Road as an early blueprint for globalization, demonstrating how commercial interdependence can drive both human innovation and shared vulnerability. Studying its rise and transformation reminds modern societies that global connectivity brings immense cultural enrichment alongside complex systemic risks.

Source: Adapted from UNESCO Silk Roads Programme & Frankopan, P., "The Silk Roads: A New History of the World," 2021.

Which of the following best restates the sentence "Paper-making and gunpowder techniques traveled westward from China, fundamentally transforming European literacy and warfare, while mathematical concepts and astronomical instruments moved eastward." in the second paragraph?', 'European inventions like paper and gunpowder moved eastward to China, where they were traded for mathematical concepts.', 'Chinese innovations in paper and gunpowder reshaped reading and military practices in Europe, whereas ideas in mathematics and astronomy flowed toward the East.', 'Mathematical concepts and astronomical instruments prevented the spread of Chinese gunpowder and paper-making to Europe.', 'Warfare in China was transformed by European astronomical instruments that arrived via maritime trade routes.', 'Literacy in Europe declined after Chinese paper-making techniques replaced traditional mathematical instruments.', 'B', 'Chinese innovations in paper and gunpowder reshaped reading and military practices in Europe, whereas ideas in mathematics and astronomy flowed toward the East.
Alasan Jawaban Benar: Menjaga arah pertukaran budaya dan teknologi dengan tepat: kertas & mesiu dari Tiongkok ke Barat (European literacy and warfare $\rightarrow$ reading and military practices in Europe), sedangkan matematika & astronomi bergerak ke Timur (moved eastward $\rightarrow$ flowed toward the East).
Perbandingan dengan Opsi Lain:
(A) Salah: Arahnya terbalik; kertas dan mesiu berasal dari Tiongkok menuju Eropa, bukan sebaliknya.
(C) Salah: Konsep matematika tidak menghalangi penyebaran mesiu dan kertas.
(D) Salah: Instrumen astronomi bergerak ke Timur, bukan mengubah peperangan di Tiongkok lewat jalur laut.
(E) Salah: Literasi di Eropa justru bertransformasi maju berkat teknologi pembuatan kertas, bukan menurun.', true),
