Which conclusion is best supported by combining information from both passages?', 'Modern lifestyle habits that sacrifice sleep directly undermine essential brain cleaning and memory processes that cannot be easily compensated for later.', 'Physiologists still consider sleep to be an unproductive period where the brain shuts down completely.', 'Shift workers are unaffected by blue light because their circadian rhythms adapt naturally within a week.', 'Alzheimer''s disease is caused solely by using smartphones before bedtime.', 'Weekend recovery sleep is scientifically proven to be more restorative than regular nightly sleep.', 'A', 'Modern lifestyle habits that sacrifice sleep directly undermine essential brain cleaning and memory processes that cannot be easily compensated for later.
Alasan Jawaban Benar: Menggabungkan kedua teks: kebiasaan modern seperti penggunaan gawai larut malam mengorbankan waktu tidur (Text 2), yang secara langsung mengganggu pembersihan racun otak dan konsolidasi memori (Text 1), serta tidak bisa ditebus begitu saja dengan tidur tambahan di akhir pekan (Text 2).
Perbandingan dengan Opsi Lain:
(B) Salah: Ahli fisiologi modern sudah meninggalkan anggapan bahwa otak mati total saat tidur.
(C) Salah: Pekerja shift justru mengalami gangguan ritme sirkadian dan penurunan kesehatan.
(D) Salah: Penumpukan protein beta-amyloid dikaitkan dengan Alzheimer, tetapi teks tidak mengklaim smartphone adalah satu-satunya penyebab Alzheimer.
(E) Salah: Tidur pengganti di akhir pekan terbukti tidak mampu memulihkan performa saraf secara optimal.
Passage 4: Standardized Testing in Education (Soshum)', true),
('e1662a27-1850-54c0-a700-001c2c9c3f23', 'LBE', 236, '07. Relation Between Texts (Hubungan Antar-Teks)', 'Sulit', 'Passage 4 (Questions 16–20) — Tema: Soshum (Sosiologi Pendidikan & Psikologi Sosial)

Text 1 Standardized testing has long been championed by educational policymakers as an objective, meritocratic tool for evaluating student achievement and allocating university admissions. By administering identical exams under uniform conditions, proponents argue that standardized assessments eliminate teacher bias and provide a transparent benchmark across diverse schools. In theory, high test scores reflect individual diligence and intellectual readiness, offering talented students from modest backgrounds a fair pathway to upward social mobility.

(Source: Adapted from Educational Policy Analysis Archives – "Meritocracy and the Case for Standardized Assessment", 2023)

Text 2 However, sociologists of education contend that standardized tests often measure socioeconomic privilege rather than innate academic potential. Wealthier families routinely invest thousands of dollars in private tutoring, specialized test-preparation courses, and multiple exam retakes—resources entirely inaccessible to low-income students. Additionally, underfunded public schools frequently lack experienced instructors and advanced curricula, placing disadvantaged students at a structural disadvantage long before exam day.

Moreover, high-stakes testing distorts classroom instruction through a phenomenon known as "teaching to the test." Under intense administrative pressure to boost school rankings, teachers narrow their curricula to rote memorization and test-taking strategies, sidelining critical thinking, creativity, and collaborative problem-solving. Consequently, critics argue that overreliance on standardized scores perpetuates educational inequality while failing to cultivate the adaptable skills demanded by modern workplaces.

(Source: Adapted from Sociology of Education Journal – "The Hidden Inequalities of High-Stakes Testing", 2025)

How does Text 2 respond to the argument presented in Text 1?', 'Text 2 challenges the claim that standardized testing is truly meritocratic by highlighting socioeconomic disparities and its narrowing effect on classroom learning.', 'Text 2 supports Text 1 by proving that low-income students score higher when teachers focus on rote memorization.', 'Text 2 agrees that private tutoring eliminates teacher bias in underfunded public schools.', 'Text 2 argues that university admissions should rely exclusively on exam retakes rather than school rankings.', 'Text 2 confirms that standardized testing is the best way to develop creativity and critical thinking.', 'A', 'Text 2 challenges the claim that standardized testing is truly meritocratic by highlighting socioeconomic disparities and its narrowing effect on classroom learning.
Alasan Jawaban Benar: Text 1 membela tes terstandar sebagai alat meritokrasi yang objektif dan adil bagi mobilitas sosial. Text 2 (Paragraf 1 & 2) membantahnya dengan menunjukkan bahwa tes tersebut lebih mencerminkan privilese ekonomi keluarga kaya serta menyempitkan kurikulum belajar di kelas.
Perbandingan dengan Opsi Lain:
(B) Salah: Text 2 mengkritik habis-habisan sistem tes terstandar, bukan mendukung Text 1.
(C) Salah: Les privat justru memperlebar ketimpangan antara siswa kaya dan miskin.
(D) Salah: Text 2 tidak menyarankan seleksi masuk universitas bergantung pada pengulangan ujian.
(E) Salah: Text 2 justru menyatakan tes terstandar mengesampingkan kreativitas dan pemikiran kritis.', true),
('f005d32f-6ee5-5cb1-bf2f-4830d7d852f7', 'LBE', 237, '07. Relation Between Texts (Hubungan Antar-Teks)', 'Sulit', 'Passage 4 (Questions 16–20) — Tema: Soshum (Sosiologi Pendidikan & Psikologi Sosial)

