How does the second paragraph relate to the first paragraph?', 'It provides further examples of the "double coincidence of wants" in classical textbooks.', 'It counters the barter myth in the first paragraph by presenting anthropological evidence that credit and mutual obligation came first.', 'It proves that clay tablets were used as physical coins in everyday village markets.', 'It argues that early villagers never helped one another during agricultural harvests.', 'It shifts the focus from ancient history to twenty-first-century central banking.', 'B', 'It counters the barter myth in the first paragraph by presenting anthropological evidence that credit and mutual obligation came first.
Alasan Jawaban Benar: Kata "Instead..." di awal Paragraf 2 menandai bantahan langsung terhadap skenario barter pada Paragraf 1, dengan menunjukkan catatan etnografis dan arkeologis bahwa masyarakat awal menggunakan sistem kredit dan utang sosial (credit and mutual obligation), bukan barter langsung.
Perbandingan dengan Opsi Lain:
(A), (C), (D), dan (E) Salah: Paragraf 2 menolak mitos double coincidence of wants, loh tanah liat (clay tablets) adalah catatan akuntansi kredit (bukan koin fisik), warga desa justru saling membantu saat panen, dan fokusnya masih pada masyarakat kuno.', true),
('e85b548f-dae4-5959-bcda-088a59ef1f64', 'LBE', 398, '15. Organization of Ideas & Paragraph Structure', 'Sulit', 'Passage 4 (Questions 16–20) — Tema: Soshum (Antropologi Ekonomi & Sejarah Uang)

According to a persistent myth popularized in classical economics textbooks, money was invented simply to overcome the inefficiencies of pure barter economies. In this hypothetical scenario, early villagers allegedly struggled with the "double coincidence of wants"—a shoemaker wanting bread had to find a baker who simultaneously needed shoes—until societies agreed to adopt precious metals as a universal medium of exchange. Despite its intuitive appeal, modern anthropologists and historians have found virtually no empirical evidence that pure barter economies ever existed as the primary mode of exchange within early human communities.

Instead, ethnographic and archaeological records from ancient Mesopotamia to indigenous societies reveal that early communities operated primarily on intricate systems of credit and mutual obligation. Rather than trading goods spot-for-spot with neighbors, villagers kept mental or clay-tablet ledgers of social debts, assisting one another during harvests or emergencies with the expectation of future reciprocity. Standardized units of account—such as weights of silver or bushels of barley—emerged thousands of years before physical coins to measure these ongoing credit relationships.

Physical coinage only appeared much later, around the seventh century BCE in kingdoms like Lydia, China, and India, largely driven by state administration and military logistics. Rulers needed a portable, standardized way to pay large standing armies of mercenaries and collect taxes across expanding territories, transforming localized social credit into impersonal, state-backed currency.

Understanding this historical trajectory reframes how we view modern financial systems. Far from being a purely natural commodity discovered in markets, money is fundamentally a social and political institution built on collective trust, legal governance, and mutual credit.

(Source: Adapted from David Graeber – "Debt: The First 5,000 Years" & Journal of Economic Anthropology, 2023)

What is the main purpose of the third paragraph?', 'To explain why physical coins were eventually invented by states to fund armies and collect taxes.', 'To argue that seventh-century BCE kingdoms relied exclusively on barter to pay soldiers.', 'To prove that silver weights and barley bushels were invented after metal coins.', 'To criticize ancient rulers for abolishing localized social credit systems.', 'To compare the artistic designs of Lydian, Chinese, and Indian coins.', 'A', 'To explain why physical coins were eventually invented by states to fund armies and collect taxes.
Alasan Jawaban Benar: Paragraf 3 berfokus menjelaskan kapan dan mengapa koin logam fisik akhirnya muncul (sekitar abad ke-7 SM di Lydia, Tiongkok, dan India), yaitu sebagai instrumen administrasi negara untuk menggaji tentara bayaran dan memungut pajak.
Perbandingan dengan Opsi Lain:
(B), (C), (D), dan (E) Salah: Kerajaan abad ke-7 SM memakai koin (bukan barter) untuk menggaji tentara, takaran perak dan jelai muncul ribuan tahun sebelum koin fisik (Paragraf 2), penguasa tidak dikritik, dan desain artistik koin tidak dibandingkan.', true),
